# wazap settings. Copy to ~/.wazap/.env (or <data-dir>/.env) and uncomment what
# you need — every setting is optional, and a real environment variable always
# wins over this file. Command-line flags win over both. Most of these are
# written for you by `wazap login`, `wazap config` and `wazap expose`.

# Where wazap keeps accounts.json, accounts/<id>/, models/ and server.lock.
# WAZAP_DATA_DIR=~/.wazap

# Refuse every write. The send/manage tools are not registered at all, so an
# agent cannot message anyone from this account even by mistake. Unset and 0
# both allow writes. 1 turns them off until `wazap config writes on` and a restart.
# Invalid values are refused. Durable per-account restrictions live in accounts.json.
# WAZAP_READ_ONLY=0

# Privacy: keep messages in the account database (accounts/<id>/wazap.sqlite)
# so they survive a restart. 0 keeps no messages on disk: every stored message is
# removed at each start and stop; chats, contacts, notes and the content-free
# deletion barriers remain, and recall is off.
# WAZAP_PERSIST_HISTORY=1

# Strict retention, off by default. On: messages marked as disappearing expire
# locally at their earliest known deadline (keep-in-chat is not an exemption, and
# a marked message with an uncomputable timer is refused). Deleted messages are
# forgotten either way. See README.
# WAZAP_RETENTION=0

# --- HTTP (`wazap serve --http`; the default is stdio) ---
# WAZAP_HOST=127.0.0.1
# WAZAP_PORT=8766

# --- OAuth, for agents that reach this server by URL (claude.ai, ChatGPT, Poke) ---
# Both together turn it on; `wazap expose` writes them. The URL is where agents
# reach this server from the outside and must be https. The password is what the
# consent page asks you for; generate with: openssl rand -base64 18
# Clients must save rotated refresh tokens; a consumed one works for 60 s more. See README.
# WAZAP_PUBLIC_URL=https://wazap.example.com
# WAZAP_OAUTH_PASSWORD=

# --- Static tokens, for your own code calling wazap (README: Building on wazap) ---
# Bearer token for GET/POST /mcp. REQUIRED to bind anything but loopback.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# WAZAP_READ_TOKEN=

# Only sessions presenting this token get the write tools, so a leaked read
# token can never message anyone. The token is not the same as writes being
# enabled: WAZAP_READ_ONLY=1 hides write tools from every session.
# WAZAP_WRITE_TOKEN=

# --- Voice transcription (`wazap config transcribe`) ---
# local runs whisper.cpp; openai uploads audio to the configured provider.
# WAZAP_TRANSCRIBE=off
# The key for openai; OPENAI_API_KEY is the fallback. Never a command-line flag.
# WAZAP_TRANSCRIBE_API_KEY=

# --- Local semantic recall (`wazap config recall`) ---
# Indexes message text with a local embedding model, so `recall` finds messages
# by meaning as well as by words, across every kept message. Needs
# llama.cpp (`brew install llama.cpp`) and a downloaded model
# (`wazap embed download`). Off when unset, and off whenever
# WAZAP_PERSIST_HISTORY=0: derived data must not outlive its source.
# WAZAP_RECALL=off

# --- Outbound webhook (inbound messages; the other events are opt-in) ---
# `wazap config webhook on` asks for the URL and secret. On without a URL or
# secret fails status, doctor and setup. An account may override the URL, secret
# and events in accounts.json (webhook_url, webhook_secret, webhook_events).
# wazap webhook test --account x uses those.
# WAZAP_WEBHOOK=off
# Redirects are refused. Query tokens are allowed; userinfo and fragments are not.
# WAZAP_WEBHOOK_URL=https://example.com/hooks/wazap
# WAZAP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Which events get posted, comma-separated, or all for every one of them.
# Unset is message_received only, so a consumer that answers every POST is
# never made to answer messages you typed on the phone. message_sent and
# connection arrive only if you ask for them here.
# WAZAP_WEBHOOK_EVENTS=message_received

# --- Advanced: a self-hosted OAuth server behind a reverse proxy ---
# OAuth proxy trust for X-Forwarded-For and rate limits. Default: loopback only.
# Use "none" when there is no reverse proxy; otherwise name only trusted proxy
# IPs/CIDRs, comma-separated (e.g. loopback,172.20.0.2/32). Not whole LANs.
# A trusted proxy MUST overwrite/sanitize incoming X-Forwarded-For. Private IPs
# are not automatically trusted. CF-Connecting-IP alone is ignored: configure
# your Cloudflare/tunnel ingress to supply a trustworthy X-Forwarded-For chain.
# Restart after changing this. This does not grant MCP or local-file access.
# WAZAP_TRUST_PROXY=loopback
